.031341552734375, 0.0018091201782226562, -0.015655517578125, 0.021240234375, 0.025299072265625, -0.0036487579345703125, 0.0079498291015625, 0.01555633544921875, 0.00032520294189453125, -0.0195770263671875, 0.0221710205078125, 0.00013518333435058594, 0.03729248046875, 0.006866455078125, 0.026031494140625, -0.0111083984375, 0.040771484375, 0.026763916015625, 0.004375457763671875, 0.0164337158203125, -0.01558685302734375, -0.0099945068359375, 0.0154266357421875, -0.00836944580078125, -0.0210723876953125, 0.0191650390625, 0.0159759521484375, 0.00925445556640625, -0.0114898681640625, -0.0015363693237304688, -0.01213836669921875, -0.006298065185546875, 0.02984619140625, -0.0169219970703125, 0.005558013916015625, -0.00614166259765625, -0.0236358642578125, 0.01474761962890625, -0.02923583984375, -0.035125732421875, 0.019256591796875, 0.0117950439453125, 0.0118560791015625, 0.0031871795654296875, 0.0303955078125, 0.030487060546875, -0.007724761962890625, -0.01406097412109375, -0.0116424560546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. A multi-agent AI pipeline was developed and deployed locally to perform report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The pipeline consisted of three primary agents, each with a stepwise workflow:\n\n1. Sectioning Agent:\n   - **Step 1:** Each sentence in the report was scanned for explicit section headers and anatomical terms using a predefined list of regular expression (regex) patterns.\n   - **Step 2:** If a sentence matched a single, unambiguous regex pattern, it was assigned to the corresponding anatomical section.\n   - **Step 3:** If a sentence matched multiple patterns, none, or if the context was ambiguous (e.g., overlapping anatomical references or implicit section transitions), the sentence was passed to a local large language model (LLM; Llama-2-13B, default parameters, no fine-tuning).\n   - **Step 4:** The LLM assigned the sentence to the most appropriate anatomical section based on contextual understanding, considering surrounding sentences and clinical context.\n   - **Step 5:** The final section assignment for each sentence prioritized regex-based assignments when unambiguous, and deferred to the LLM only when regex rules were inconclusive or conflicting.\n   - **Example:** If a sentence mentioned \"pleural effusion\" without a clear section header and regex could not resolve the section, the LLM would use context to assign it to the \"Pleura\" section.\n\n2. Consistency Agent:\n   - **Step 1:** Identified key findings (e.g., \"pulmonary embolism,\" \"liver lesion\") using a curated list of clinical terms.\n   - **Step 2:** Checked for the presence or absence of these findings in both Findings and Impression sections.\n   - **Step 3:** Flagged discrepancies if a key finding appeared in one section but not the other, or if terminology or anatomical references were inconsistent (e.g., \"right renal mass\" in Findings but \"left renal mass\" in Impression).\n   - **Step 4:** Discrepancies were defined as any mismatch in laterality, organ, or critical finding between sections.\n   - **Step 5:** Rules were iteratively refined using a development set of 50 reports, with manual review to adjust term lists and logic for improved sensitivity and specificity.\n\n3. QA Agent:\n   - **Step 1:** Identified gender-anatomy conflicts (e.g., references to male anatomy in female patients) using keyword matching (e.g., searching for \"prostate\" in female patients).\n   - **Step 2:** Used LLM-based contextual analysis to flag potential issues not captured by keywords.\n   - **Step 3:** For critical findings, checked for the presence of communication documentation (e.g., \"results communicated to referring physician\") when a critical finding was detected.\n\nThe agents operated sequentially: the Sectioning Agent output fed into the Consistency Agent, followed by the QA Agent. Each report passed through all agents, and flagged issues were aggregated for review.\n\nEvaluation Subset Selection: A subset of 45 reports was selected for independent evaluation by two board-certified radiologists. Stratified random sampling was used to ensure proportional representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, and undocumented critical findings) and unflagged reports. The full dataset was divided into strata based on the presence and type of QA flags, and reports were randomly sampled from each stratum in proportion to their prevalence in the full dataset. This approach ensured that each flagged error type and unflagged reports were represented in the evaluation subset according to their frequency, while also guaranteeing inclusion of rare error types. The subset size of 45 was chosen to balance feasibility of detailed manual review with the need to include sufficient examples of each error type, given resource constraints and the distribution of errors in the dataset. While this sample size allowed for initial performance characterization and inclusion of rare errors, it may limit statistical power and the precision of confidence intervals.\n\nEvaluation Metrics: \n- **Operational Definitions:** \n  - \"Correctly restructured\" was defined as the report being segmented into anatomical sections such that each sentence was assigned to the appropriate section without altering the original clinical content or meaning. Specifically, each sentence about a particular anatomical structure (e.g., \"hepatic steatosis\") should appear only in the corresponding section (e.g., \"Liver\"), and no sentences should be omitted, duplicated, or moved to an incorrect section. \n  - \"Incorrectly restructured\" indicated that the restructuring introduced errors in section assignment (e.g., a lung finding placed in the \"Liver\" section), omitted sentences, or altered the intended meaning (e.g., splitting a sentence so that its clinical context was lost).\n  - **Reviewer Rubric:** Reviewers were provided with a rubric specifying that: (a) all sentences must be present and assigned to the correct anatomical section; (b) no sentences should be assigned to multiple sections; (c) the clinical meaning of each sentence must be preserved; and (d) any deviation from these criteria constituted an incorrect restructuring. Example: If the sentence \"There is a right lower lobe nodule\" appeared in the \"Liver\" section, this was scored as incorrect. If the sentence \"Mild hepatic steatosis\" was correctly placed in the \"Liver\" section, this was scored as correct.\n- QA performance ratings were assigned on a 3-point scale: \"excellent\" (all relevant issues correctly flagged, no errors or omissions), \"good\" (minor errors or omissions not affecting clinical interpretation, such as a missed typographical inconsistency), and \"fair\" (errors or omissions with potential to affect clinical interpretation, such as missing a critical finding or misclassifying a major discrepancy). Reviewers received standardized written instructions and a training session with example cases to calibrate their assessments. For each report, reviewers independently recorded their judgments according to these definitions.\n\nStatistical Analysis: Reviewer agreement was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic. For key proportions (e.g., percentage of correctly restructured reports, QA performance ratings), 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated using the Wilson score interval. For QA flagging, precision, recall, and F1-score were calculated for each error type (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, undocumented critical findings) using reviewer consensus as the reference standard. The primary outcome was the proportion of reports correctly restructured by the system, as determined by reviewer consensus. Secondary outcomes included the accuracy of QA flagging (true/false positives and false negatives for each error type), precision, recall, F1-score, and inter-rater agreement statistics. Effect sizes were not calculated because the study was descriptive and not designed to compare interventions or groups; the focus was on system performance characterization.\n\nResults:\n\nSystem Performance\n\n- The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining the original report content.\n- The system flagged 90 reports (14.1%) for at least one QA issue:\n    - Section mismatches: 80 reports (12.5%)\n    - Gender-anatomy conflicts: 7 reports (1.1%)\n    - Undocumented communication of critical findings: 3 reports (0.5%)\n- In the full dataset, of the 80 reports flagged for section mismatches, 75 were confirmed as true positives and 5 as false positives by reviewer consensus. Of the 7 reports flagged for gender-anatomy conflicts, 6 were true positives and 1 was a false positive. All 3 reports flagged for undocumented communication of critical findings were true positives.\n- **False Negatives:** In the 45-report evaluation subset, reviewers identified 2 section mismatches and 1 gender-anatomy conflict that were not flagged by the system, representing false negatives. No false negatives were identified for undocumented communication of critical findings in the evaluation subset. Extrapolating to the full dataset, the false negative rate may be underestimated due to the small sample size.\n- **QA Flagging Metrics (Evaluation Subset):**\n    - Section mismatches: Precision 93.8%, Recall 97.4%, F1-score 95.6%\n    - Gender-anatomy conflicts: Precision 85.7%, Recall 85.7%, F1-score 85.7%\n    - Undocumented communication of critical findings: Precision 100%, Recall 100%, F1-score 100%\n\nRadiologist Evaluation\n\n- Of the 45-report evaluation subset, both reviewers agreed that 31 reports (69%; 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 reports (4%; 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and they disagreed on 12 reports (27%; 95% CI: 15\u201341%). Inter-rater agreement for restructuring was moderate (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.56, p < 0.001).\n- For QA flagging, both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced in any report.\n- QA performance ratings were \"excellent\" in 24 reports (53%; 95% CI: 38\u201368%), \"good\" in 14 reports (31%; 95% CI: 18\u201346%), and \"fair\" in 7 reports (16%; 95% CI: 7\u201329%). Inter-rater agreement for QA ratings was substantial (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.72, p < 0.001).\n\nDetailed QA Task Results\n\n- Of the 7 reports flagged for gender-anatomy conflicts, both reviewers confirmed 6 as true positives and 1 as a false positive"}}